where it is written
  ok   the transcript is a graph beside the exploration, not inside it
  ok   which is what keeps the server's next rewrite from erasing it

what is kept
  ok   every event that carried content becomes an exchange, in order
  ok   numbered by the order they happened
  ok   the payload is kept whole, not summarised
  ok   and its stated cardinality is read out for the indicator
  ok   with which way it moved, and what was asked
  ok   the schema half -- the documents the model read -- is kept as well
  ok   the model's prose is kept, with what the turn cost

an oversized payload
  ok   is cut, and says both what was kept and what there was

the update it writes
  ok   one INSERT DATA, in the server's own vocabulary
  ok   into the transcript graph
  ok   pointing back at the exploration it belongs to
  ok   quotes and backslashes in the payload are escaped, not dropped
  ok   and newlines travel as escapes, so one payload is one literal
  ok   which is what the escaper does
  ok   an IRI that would break the update is refused rather than escaped

writing it
  ok   the run is recorded
  ok   into the account's exploration repository, where the log already lives
  ok   as a SPARQL update, which the graph-store resource decodes
  ok   under the user's own token
  ok   and the body carries the payload

when the store will not take it
  ok   the failure is returned, not thrown -- the run already happened

a run with no exploration handle
  ok   is still recorded, under a graph of its own rather than discarded
  ok   with nothing to point at, and no pretence that there is

reading it back
  ok   the runs are separated from their exchanges
  ok   a run carries what was asked of it
  ok   its exchanges come back in the order they happened, not the rows'
  ok   and the payload reads back exactly as it was written
  ok   a document the model read keeps its name without overwriting the record's own
  ok   and the header says which model, against which exploration

